## 3. The operational case — for and against

### For the thesis

Specifying acoustic performance, PA zoning, and announcement governance in advance of design saves the operator from three recurring failure modes I have watched play out on other capital handoffs:

- Terminals delivered with reverberation times in the 2.5–4.0 second range that make the required NFPA 72 STI unreachable without post-occupancy foam panels, adding cost and disfiguring the design intent.
- PA systems zoned by architectural convenience rather than operating logic — a single zone covering three hold-rooms because the ceiling grid was already laid out — which forces gate agents to broadcast their gate calls into two neighboring gates and produces the noise pollution the standard is meant to prevent.
- FIDS wiring and screen placement designed by a low-voltage subcontractor working off a room-count schedule, with no operational review of sightlines, screen density at decision points, or fallback for outage.

Each of these is a Monday-morning problem I did not create and cannot fix without a nine-figure retrofit. Writing the requirements down before schematic design is the cheapest intervention in the entire lifecycle. The Concourse E successors and the C/D replacement are the last chance in a generation to get this right at IAD.

The standard also gives me an enforcement instrument I currently do not have. When a signatory airline installs a boarding-area TV at 78 dBA measured at seated ear level, or a concessionaire runs a coffee grinder that swamps the neighboring holdroom PA, my only recourse today is an OPSCOMM call and a request. A design-manual reference incorporated by the U&L and tenant handbook gives me a specification to point at.

### Against the thesis

The strategist should hear this bluntly: a standard written by people who have not stood on the C concourse at 6:00 AM during a Nor'easter push will make the airport worse.

- **The "reduce announcements" instinct is the wrong headline.** Announcement volume is a symptom of PA zone geometry and gate-agent behavior. Fixing the zones changes the number of announcements a passenger hears without changing the number of announcements the system carries. A standard that starts from "fewer announcements" will produce compliance theater — quieter routine operations paired with worse intelligibility in IROPS.
- **The accessibility exposure is asymmetric.** If we get quiet-by-design wrong, the passengers we harm first are older travelers, non-English speakers, hearing-impaired passengers using assistive listening, and infrequent travelers without the app. Those are the passengers whose complaints reach the Board. The redundant-channel requirement is not an ADA formality; it is what protects the standard from becoming a political liability.
- **The IROPS override cannot be an afterthought.** A silent-airport rulebook that has to be suspended every time a thunderstorm rolls through the DCA area is a rulebook that gets suspended most peak-day afternoons in July. The standard needs one state, not two.
- **Enforcement against airlines is a U&L problem.** The 2025 agreement did not include this. Any real teeth against carrier-side behavior — script content, gate-agent PA discipline, common-use system configuration — will require negotiation and probably concession. Do not pretend the design manual alone is enough.
- **The post-2025 DCA political environment is unforgiving.** After the January 29, 2025 midair collision, any standard perceived to reduce safety-related communication at DCA will be litigated in the press before it is litigated in the courts. The standard must be able to demonstrate — with STI numbers, with coverage maps, with EAP integration language — that emergency communication is enhanced, not diminished.

